+   buffer must NOT be one contiguous std::string: libc++'s basic_string grows
+   by exact doubling (max(2*old_cap, new_size)), so appending a large payload
+   one push at a time eventually issues ONE reallocation sized to the whole
+   buffer so far — a single, often non-power-of-two, high-order buddy-block
+   request. This is exactly what #208 hit trying to substitute `guest-clang`:
+   an observed 134,352,896 B allocation, order-16, that std::bad_alloc'd on
+   the guest's NOMMU allocator (which routinely has several 128 MiB blocks
+   free but none at 256 MiB) — and nix reported it as the misleading "no
+   substituter that can build it", three layers removed from the actual
+   allocator failure. See each function's own comment below for exactly
+   which of its two buffers (compressed vs. uncompressed) is the one #208's
+   own numbers point at — they are not the same buffer, and got conflated
+   in an earlier pass at this fix.
